1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Exciting architecture, Dome Stadiums on Modern Marvels.

2
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,440
Great spectacles can stir the soul.

3
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:28,400
They reduce epic battles into refined theatrical struggles.

4
00:00:28,401 --> 00:00:39,150
It is life in miniature performed for the amusement of the masses, who for the length of the game can share the glory of the victorious without suffering the effort of

5
00:00:39,151 --> 00:00:40,180
the achievement.

6
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:50,380
The stadium concept has its origins in antiquity in the Greek and Roman periods.

7
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:54,420
Prior to that, games did not exist,

8
00:00:54,421 --> 00:01:00,140
and it was the Greeks who built the first stadia for horse racing and so on.

9
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:02,960
And then the Romans, of course, went much further

10
00:01:02,961 --> 00:01:08,880
because they used it as a method of pacifying their populations,

11
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:12,080
especially in places like the Roman Colosseum,

12
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:17,320
where they did a lot of killing of people for entertainment purposes.

13
00:01:17,321 --> 00:01:24,320
Early sporting spectacles often climaxed with a big finish for the second place contestant,

14
00:01:24,321 --> 00:01:28,320
a practice which has largely been abandoned in recent times.

15
00:01:28,321 --> 00:01:31,320
Modern sports are rarely this bloodthirsty.

16
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:40,320
But throughout the 20th century, Americans have been equally passionate about winning and losing.

17
00:01:40,321 --> 00:01:45,320
Our fans revel in projecting their dreams into their heroes,

18
00:01:45,321 --> 00:01:49,920
imagining it was themselves swatting the hickory against horsehide

19
00:01:49,921 --> 00:01:52,860
to send the tomatoes screaming out at a park

20
00:01:52,861 --> 00:01:55,880
to win a one-way ticket to the record books.

21
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:04,880
Baseball emerged in the 1800s as a rural pastime.

22
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:07,660
It quickly spread across America,

23
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:11,120
jumping all lines of religion, color, or class.

24
00:02:11,121 --> 00:02:17,160
The nation became intoxicated by the game of teamwork and forgiveness

25
00:02:17,161 --> 00:02:21,560
in which you can steal a base, make an error, take three swings every turn

26
00:02:21,561 --> 00:02:25,920
run into an open field and have a crowd roar when you stride home

27
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:30,440
Religion reinforces our faith in a higher power

28
00:02:30,441 --> 00:02:34,360
The ballpark reinforces our faith in ourselves

29
00:02:34,361 --> 00:02:40,480
In their times, Babe Ruth, Lou Gehrig, Mickey Mantle and Willie Mays were almost gods

30
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:43,100
Bats and balls were holy icons.

31
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,000
Hot dogs, a sacrament.

32
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:47,100
Home runs, an epiphany.

33
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:49,480
The stadium, a cathedral.

34
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:55,580
Because of cold weather and winter storms, baseball was only played in summer.

35
00:02:55,581 --> 00:03:00,400
But even then, searing heat and sudden showers could quickly cut the heart out of a game.

36
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:08,400
Likewise, other spectacle sports like football could also be seasonally disadvantaged.

37
00:03:08,401 --> 00:03:13,620
and the cost of building and maintaining large stadiums demanded year-round use.

38
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:18,740
It was a very human notion to consider an artificial environment

39
00:03:18,741 --> 00:03:21,400
to house big spectacles from the elements

40
00:03:21,401 --> 00:03:25,540
and it was a very American notion to accept the incredible scale

41
00:03:25,541 --> 00:03:27,740
which would be demanded in such a project.

42
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:32,080
A dimension achieved in such accomplishments as Boulder Dam

43
00:03:32,081 --> 00:03:33,880
and the skyscrapers of Manhattan.

44
00:03:33,881 --> 00:03:39,880
The challenge of a structure's purpose often defines its design.

45
00:03:39,881 --> 00:03:43,880
As architect Louis Sullivan noted, form follows function.

46
00:03:43,881 --> 00:03:51,880
In putting a roof over a stadium, there would be no place to position support columns without disrupting the action of a game.

47
00:03:51,881 --> 00:03:59,880
The answer is an arched roof, which needs no columns because its weight is diverted laterally and supported by the building's walls.

48
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:00,581
walls.

49
00:04:00,582 --> 00:04:05,260
Those walls then need buttresses to keep from buckling under the increased load

50
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:10,093
and further dissipate the overall weight. This is not a new idea.

51
00:04:10,094 --> 00:04:11,580
The concept has been

52
00:04:11,581 --> 00:04:16,340
used for centuries in the construction of Gothic cathedrals where great height could

53
00:04:16,341 --> 00:04:20,444
not be accommodated through the support of columns.

54
00:04:20,445 --> 00:04:23,260
The 20th century approach, however,

55
00:04:23,261 --> 00:04:26,748
recalls earlier structures based on a dome design.

56
00:04:26,749 --> 00:04:29,120
In this situation, the ceiling and

57
00:04:29,121 --> 00:04:34,040
the walls were a single unit and the entire weight of the building flows evenly to the

58
00:04:34,041 --> 00:04:41,040
ground, equally distributing the load over its 360 degrees. Likewise, the gravity of

59
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:46,430
the sides meets in the middle, making a center support unnecessary.

60
00:04:46,431 --> 00:04:48,160
The shape also results

61
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:54,540
in energy conservation as thermal waves are both reflected and circulated by the curved

62
00:04:54,541 --> 00:04:55,760
interior surface.

63
00:04:55,761 --> 00:05:01,360
The antecedents of the dome is the fact that it's the simplest way of covering

64
00:05:01,361 --> 00:05:03,797
a space if it's not too big.

65
00:05:03,798 --> 00:05:09,540
And you have the Inuit ice house and the notion of shelter used in

66
00:05:09,541 --> 00:05:13,710
buildings made of vegetation, made of wood, sod.

67
00:05:13,711 --> 00:05:17,880
As the appeal of modern team sports continued to

68
00:05:17,881 --> 00:05:22,687
grow, so did the need for domed stadiums to facilitate year-round events.

69
00:05:22,688 --> 00:05:23,740
The only missing

70
00:05:23,741 --> 00:05:25,137
ingredient was money.

71
00:05:25,138 --> 00:05:30,060
But America had plenty of that in the second half of the 1900s, especially

72
00:05:30,061 --> 00:05:30,807
in Texas.

73
00:05:30,808 --> 00:05:37,200
It had been a flat, barren frontier where grazing herds of steer roamed free.

74
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:44,260
Then vast amounts of oil were discovered underground in the 1930s, and the landscape changed.

75
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:52,600
Cattlemen, gunslingers, and outlaws were replaced by a more dangerous breed, oilmen, accountants,

76
00:05:52,601 --> 00:05:53,720
and lawyers.

77
00:05:53,721 --> 00:06:00,720
By the 1960s, sports fans in hot and humid Houston were demanding an indoor

78
00:06:00,721 --> 00:06:04,765
air-conditioned arena, and the Astrodome was born.

79
00:06:04,766 --> 00:06:07,920
The Astrodome was state-of-the-art when

80
00:06:07,921 --> 00:06:12,760
it became the very first dome stadium, but the self-proclaimed eighth wonder of the world

81
00:06:12,761 --> 00:06:20,260
had not worked out all the bugs before it opened in 1965. The first controversy was

82
00:06:20,261 --> 00:06:22,610
the giant facility's air conditioning system.

83
00:06:22,611 --> 00:06:24,960
During its inaugural season, the visiting New

84
00:06:24,961 --> 00:06:30,120
York Mets claimed that the Astros were using Dome's cooling system to create wind currents

85
00:06:30,121 --> 00:06:34,235
which helped lift and carry the home team's long balls.

86
00:06:34,236 --> 00:06:36,480
Another problem arose from the

87
00:06:36,481 --> 00:06:41,780
thousands of giant glass panels in the ceiling, designed to let the sunlight spill into the

88
00:06:41,781 --> 00:06:42,788
giant structure.

89
00:06:42,789 --> 00:06:47,200
Unfortunately, they provided insufficient light to allow grass to grow

90
00:06:47,201 --> 00:06:48,234
on the field.

91
00:06:48,235 --> 00:06:54,200
Likewise, looking straight up at the panels created a strange kaleidoscopic

92
00:06:54,860 --> 00:07:00,045
glare, a definite handicap for a player trying to catch a pop fly ball.

93
00:07:00,046 --> 00:07:01,360
Painting the clear

94
00:07:01,361 --> 00:07:07,320
glass panels white seemed to lessen the disorienting optical effect, but the acres of dead playing

95
00:07:07,321 --> 00:07:10,384
field grass demanded a clever new approach.

96
00:07:10,385 --> 00:07:13,520
The solution was a thick ruffled plastic mat

97
00:07:13,521 --> 00:07:15,260
laid on the hard concrete floor.

98
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:18,280
It was called AstroTurf after the stadium,

99
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:22,920
and its slick synthetic texture sent many players out of the game

100
00:07:22,921 --> 00:07:25,460
with permanent leg, knee, and ankle injuries

101
00:07:25,461 --> 00:07:29,960
due to the inconsistent way their cleats would grip the plastic mesh.

102
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:32,700
There are drawbacks.

103
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:36,080
Physically, I think it beats up players when they have to play on it on a daily basis.

104
00:07:36,820 --> 00:07:39,820
Underneath this artificial surface is concrete.

105
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:42,760
There's no padding. It's built into the turf itself.

106
00:07:42,761 --> 00:07:46,500
So it is hard, and it does pound on you.

107
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:54,080
Artificial sod technology had improved a bit by the introduction of Seattle's Kingdome in 1971.

108
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:59,240
Management decided on an opaque ceiling instead of Houston Astrodome's glass.

109
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:07,080
But after two decades of wear and tear, the dome's interior acoustical panels have occasionally suffered from a loosening grip,

110
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:10,020
requiring ongoing and costly maintenance.

111
00:08:10,021 --> 00:08:17,100
The process of taking down these tiles and blasting away the glue spots left behind is a serious complication.

112
00:08:18,340 --> 00:08:23,920
Still a major stadium can be an attractive device to pump new life into a depressed city.

113
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:29,180
That was the case in New Orleans in 1971 when work began on the Superdome.

114
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:38,520
Stretching over 52 acres of old train yards, it was designed to boost tourism by attracting major sporting, entertainment and political events.

115
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:43,560
The Superdome featured the engineering marvel of a domed roof as its hook.

116
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:48,980
20,000 tons of steel raised the dome 273 feet over the playing field,

117
00:08:48,981 --> 00:08:52,920
so great a height that if the Statue of Liberty made her home in the outfield,

118
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:54,900
her torch would just scrape the ceiling.

119
00:08:55,500 --> 00:09:00,440
The building is so enormous inside that six stupendous television screens are suspended

120
00:09:00,441 --> 00:09:05,560
from a center overhead gondola to allow spectators a good view of the action.

121
00:09:05,561 --> 00:09:14,060
cost overruns and inflation help bring the final price tag of the superdome to 163 million dollars

122
00:09:14,061 --> 00:09:20,880
it succeeded in amazing the locals and bringing in tourists but its size and cost overruns resulted

123
00:09:20,881 --> 00:09:28,440
in a five and a half million dollar operating loss in each of its first 10 years but ill-conceived

124
00:09:28,441 --> 00:09:33,651
municipal dome stadiums can also take a political toll.

125
00:09:33,652 --> 00:09:37,440
Montreal 1976, home for the 21st Olympic

126
00:09:37,441 --> 00:09:42,880
Games and a whimsical approach to a retractable rough stadium in an environment plagued by

127
00:09:42,881 --> 00:09:44,359
harsh winter storms.

128
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:49,240
Here is a structure that is only partially domed, with the balance

129
00:09:49,241 --> 00:09:52,973
of the task performed by a gigantic umbrella.

130
00:09:52,974 --> 00:09:56,540
Twenty-six cables drape a 50-ton cover over

131
00:09:56,541 --> 00:10:02,163
the stadium from the tallest incline tower in the world, over 50 stories high.

132
00:10:02,164 --> 00:10:02,864
In 1992,

133
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:07,149
this tent covering collapsed under tons of accumulated snow.

134
00:10:07,150 --> 00:10:08,720
The French architect who

135
00:10:08,721 --> 00:10:13,940
designed the facility had been unfamiliar with Montreal's winter environment and hadn't

136
00:10:13,941 --> 00:10:16,706
added the weight in his equations.

137
00:10:16,707 --> 00:10:21,180
Soon after the accident, the administration of Montreal

138
00:10:21,181 --> 00:10:26,240
Mayor Drapeau also collapsed, a casualty of the calamity.

139
00:10:27,820 --> 00:10:30,100
Considering the mounting costs and concerns,

140
00:10:30,300 --> 00:10:33,300
it seemed unlikely that anyone would completely succeed

141
00:10:33,301 --> 00:10:35,460
in the effort to build a dome stadium.

142
00:10:35,461 --> 00:10:41,180
But in 1987, the city of Toronto embarked on a bold new approach.

143
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:45,700
Here, simplicity and ingenuity would join hands

144
00:10:45,701 --> 00:10:48,440
and inspire the ultimate arena.

145
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:57,020
Only one baseball player has ever hit the roof of the Astrodome during a game.

146
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:01,280
Mike Schmidt of the Philadelphia Phillies on June 10, 1974.

147
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:06,320
Dome Stadiums will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

148
00:11:08,580 --> 00:11:13,180
We now continue with Dome Stadiums on Modern Marvels.

149
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:16,820
I've always said it wasn't the worst stadium in baseball.

150
00:11:17,100 --> 00:11:18,260
It was the worst stadium in sports.

151
00:11:18,261 --> 00:11:22,520
What they did was take a bad football stadium, make it a very bad baseball stadium.

152
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:28,580
Exhibition Stadium was the official name for Toronto's Major League Arena,

153
00:11:28,860 --> 00:11:32,360
but most fans referred to it as the mistake by the lake.

154
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:35,780
It was a retrofit of an old football stadium.

155
00:11:36,220 --> 00:11:38,840
We built it in the wrong location.

156
00:11:39,480 --> 00:11:42,760
We knew that the traffic patterns were awful, the sight lines were awful,

157
00:11:42,761 --> 00:11:48,740
but at least it fitted the bill temporarily before we could build what we really wanted to build.

158
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:54,760
In 1986, the city of Toronto was ready to commit itself to an ambitious new stadium,

159
00:11:55,280 --> 00:12:02,020
which would invigorate the downtown area, excite sports fans, and propel the city into the future.

160
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:06,920
We thought that if we could do something that Houston was able to accomplish,

161
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:10,620
or the city of Seattle itself, we'd be going a long way.

162
00:12:10,621 --> 00:12:19,100
As we started down the road of a dome stadium, it became apparent that a lot of the baseball players prefer to play outdoors.

163
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:24,940
In fact, Buck Martinez, one of the Blue Jay catchers at the time, took me off into the corner and said,

164
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:27,800
Congratulations on getting a stadium through. I think that's great.

165
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:32,100
But from a player's standpoint, I don't think players are going to like playing indoors.

166
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:39,720
Well, I guess I was sort of horrified by the fact that here I'd been campaigning since 1969 to build a dome stadium.

167
00:12:39,721 --> 00:12:43,840
Here we are getting ready and finally in the 80s to build a stadium.

168
00:12:44,080 --> 00:12:48,840
And I was finally learning for the first time that maybe the players didn't want to play inside.

169
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:51,800
Then I suggested a retractable roof.

170
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:55,260
I said, boy, that would be the best of both worlds because you have the weather problem in Toronto.

171
00:12:56,040 --> 00:12:58,100
You would alleviate all of those concerns.

172
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:00,360
You'd be able to play in the perfect conditions all the time.

173
00:13:00,380 --> 00:13:04,920
But when it was nice in Toronto, you could open a door and you would have a convertible stadium.

174
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:09,640
So we quickly went back to the drawing board and said, look, if we're going to do something in Toronto,

175
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:11,860
we should be doing something really unique.

176
00:13:12,460 --> 00:13:22,280
In March of 83, the government announced that it was going to look into building a covered stadium for the Toronto area.

177
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:29,320
And I went after the mayor of Toronto, Mr. Eggleton, who was then mayor,

178
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:33,120
asked him for the job and he said, you and 40,000 others.

179
00:13:33,121 --> 00:13:37,240
Robbie quickly joined forces with his old friend Michael Allen,

180
00:13:37,241 --> 00:13:41,640
an engineer with the ingenuity to develop a new kind of kinetic building.

181
00:13:42,200 --> 00:13:45,840
Basically, the question that Rod put to us was,

182
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:49,700
do you want to waste a lot of time, a lot of money trying to go after a dream?

183
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:53,220
And we said, well, why not? This is a wonderful idea. Let's go after it.

184
00:13:53,820 --> 00:13:58,680
And we originally treated this as, in essence, an academic exercise.

185
00:13:58,900 --> 00:14:02,020
Let's see if we can solve the basic problem.

186
00:14:02,180 --> 00:14:06,180
How do you get a retractable roof to work for a dome structure?

187
00:14:06,181 --> 00:14:11,840
and have the roof, when it is retracted, remain on the building footprint

188
00:14:11,841 --> 00:14:13,800
and not parked on someone else's property.

189
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:17,700
And we said, what a wonderful thing to get into and see if we can solve that problem.

190
00:14:18,200 --> 00:14:22,520
It was a case of David and Goliath, with Robbie and Alan putting up their own homes

191
00:14:22,521 --> 00:14:27,820
to raise the capital to design a proposal which would rival the well-funded competition.

192
00:14:28,440 --> 00:14:32,060
But here was a challenge which demanded more imagination than money.

193
00:14:32,061 --> 00:14:36,340
They began with sketches which suggested a general aesthetic theme,

194
00:14:36,940 --> 00:14:40,720
a curved arcing form reminiscent of classic dome structures.

195
00:14:41,100 --> 00:14:45,120
But this timeless approach only solved half the equation.

196
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:49,940
A dome by itself was not enough,

197
00:14:50,460 --> 00:14:53,580
and this particularly came from the baseball crowd

198
00:14:53,581 --> 00:14:58,980
who insisted they wanted to be able to watch baseball in an open-air stadium

199
00:14:58,981 --> 00:15:00,060
when the weather is nice.

200
00:15:00,061 --> 00:15:03,660
You want to sit out and enjoy the weather, have a beer and enjoy the game.

201
00:15:04,760 --> 00:15:08,540
The answer was as close as the nearest seafood restaurant.

202
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:17,380
The inspiration was the idea of crustacean, all these shells moving, say, on a lobster or some sea animal of that kind.

203
00:15:17,780 --> 00:15:23,400
Using the appetizing notion of a lobster's hard, reticulating body plates as an inspiration,

204
00:15:24,320 --> 00:15:27,820
Robbie and Alan tested their theory through computer simulations.

205
00:15:27,821 --> 00:15:34,820
In this way, they could both illustrate and test a totally original concept in a telescoping structure.

206
00:15:34,821 --> 00:15:41,820
Two of these panels move in a linear motion, and one of the panels moves on a circular track.

207
00:15:41,821 --> 00:15:50,820
And when the retraction is completed, all of the panels stack one top of the other in a telescoping form.

208
00:15:50,821 --> 00:15:54,820
From the computer model, a miniature was assembled.

209
00:15:54,821 --> 00:16:00,460
assembled. Just like early mud huts and igloos, here was a structurally sound

210
00:16:00,461 --> 00:16:07,960
unit. The basic stress flows like water down a stream. It is an arch and the load

211
00:16:07,961 --> 00:16:13,000
flows down through the arch to the to the supports and into the concrete

212
00:16:13,001 --> 00:16:22,480
structure. This roof span is over 700 feet and the roof structure is about 15

213
00:16:22,481 --> 00:16:28,295
feet in depth. The roof is designed to support about 600 tons.

214
00:16:28,296 --> 00:16:30,640
We could support 30 DC-9s

215
00:16:30,641 --> 00:16:35,003
from the roof, providing they could all fit in there.

216
00:16:35,004 --> 00:16:37,720
The roof opens so that 91% of the

217
00:16:37,721 --> 00:16:40,480
seats in virtually all of the field is open to the sky.

218
00:16:41,200 --> 00:16:46,220
There were concerns that every fan would enjoy a direct view of all the action in a game.

219
00:16:46,221 --> 00:16:51,100
The fans wanted to be as close as possible to the players and vice versa,

220
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:54,800
and that the stadium should have an intimacy.

221
00:16:56,120 --> 00:17:01,120
Once again, computer models simulated the sight lines from every seat in the stadium,

222
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:05,320
and once again, the designers employed a practical test as well.

223
00:17:07,180 --> 00:17:13,140
They had a stadium with a roof on it, and then they would take a lamp around to act like the sun,

224
00:17:13,260 --> 00:17:15,280
and they'd watch the shadows inside the stadium.

225
00:17:15,281 --> 00:17:17,880
So that's how the process developed.

226
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:21,580
It had to have a front and a back, and it had to face the sun.

227
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:26,520
It was going to open up to let the sun come in, so it should retract to the north.

228
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:35,340
And when you saw it, its movements should be complex, elegant, and intriguing.

229
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,520
To sell the concept to government officials and decision makers,

230
00:17:39,521 --> 00:17:41,920
Robbie and Alan created a sales video

231
00:17:41,921 --> 00:17:45,540
which filtered the technical specifications of the project

232
00:17:45,541 --> 00:17:48,240
through a light-hearted, family-oriented comedy

233
00:17:48,241 --> 00:17:51,380
about a faceless designer of the dome and his children.

234
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:55,200
Dad, is this thing going to work?

235
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:57,280
Even my own son didn't believe.

236
00:17:57,760 --> 00:17:58,740
Come over here, kid.

237
00:17:59,140 --> 00:18:01,960
You're going to learn everything about this stadium.

238
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:04,800
The video worked.

239
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:07,800
Robbie and Alan won the construction contract.

240
00:18:07,801 --> 00:18:14,020
and now it was up to stadium management to fund the $210 million estimated cost.

241
00:18:16,700 --> 00:18:21,020
In metropolitan Toronto, put up $30 million towards the stadium.

242
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:24,720
The Ontario government also put up another $30 million.

243
00:18:25,400 --> 00:18:29,820
The balance was put up $150 million at that time

244
00:18:29,821 --> 00:18:35,020
by 30 private sector companies putting up $5 million each.

245
00:18:35,620 --> 00:18:40,380
Ironically, as groundbreaking ceremonies commenced on June 1, 1987,

246
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:47,020
Robbie had to admit that he was less than familiar with the games which would be played in the futuristic dome.

247
00:18:47,700 --> 00:18:52,120
When I got involved in Skydome, I'd never been to a baseball game.

248
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:56,260
I learned what baseball was as if it was nuclear physics.

249
00:18:57,240 --> 00:19:02,280
Instead, Robbie broadened the attitude of the project and gave it a deeper meaning.

250
00:19:02,281 --> 00:19:08,820
It seemed to me that this building was going to be more than just a sports park.

251
00:19:09,520 --> 00:19:13,500
It was a multifunctional building.

252
00:19:14,220 --> 00:19:22,200
And I coined the word secular cathedral simply to say it was very big and it belonged to everybody.

253
00:19:22,720 --> 00:19:26,860
The ancient Roman Colosseum would fit comfortably within the Sky Dome.

254
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:30,880
Dome stadiums will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

255
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:37,000
We now return to Dome Stadiums on Modern Marvels.

256
00:19:37,160 --> 00:19:40,780
The Blue Jays had to open on a given date, at a given time,

257
00:19:41,360 --> 00:19:44,200
and there was no forgiveness. It had to do it.

258
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:50,660
From the very beginning, project management focused on building team spirit first and a Dome second.

259
00:19:51,440 --> 00:19:58,200
A 360-degree club was initiated with hats that kept the broad vision of the endeavor on everyone's mind.

260
00:19:58,201 --> 00:20:03,520
A unity of purpose quickly spread through the team.

261
00:20:03,980 --> 00:20:08,980
Ellis Don had a constant crew of about 650 on the site.

262
00:20:09,560 --> 00:20:12,720
They worked 20 hours a day, six days a week.

263
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:19,860
At the end, we had about 2,200 people working on the job to bring it to its final completion.

264
00:20:20,600 --> 00:20:25,320
We figured that there were around 5,000 people involved in this building.

265
00:20:25,321 --> 00:20:32,520
The intense effort to meet completion deadlines rivaled the extraordinary scope of the undertaking.

266
00:20:33,220 --> 00:20:36,420
The dome was designed to be taller than a 31-story building,

267
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:45,300
large enough to house eight 747s, a herd of 751 elephants, or a 32-home suburban community.

268
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:51,340
Even the original Roman Coliseum could easily fit on the playing field.

269
00:20:51,341 --> 00:20:52,977
It was 24 hours a day.

270
00:20:52,978 --> 00:21:00,120
We did all the concrete off-site and brought it in at nighttime and assembled it in the stadium.

271
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:09,060
The basic structure was composed of over 210,000 tons of concrete with 15,000 tons of reinforcing steel.

272
00:21:09,760 --> 00:21:17,380
Many sections were prefabricated and trucked in after dark when empty roads could accommodate an endless parade of oversized trucks.

273
00:21:17,381 --> 00:21:24,380
It was important to work at a frantic pace because everyone realized that there were seasons

274
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:29,660
that Toronto's harsh climate could slow the effort to an agonizing crawl.

275
00:21:35,880 --> 00:21:40,740
It was Toronto's bitterly cold winters which took the greatest toll on construction

276
00:21:40,741 --> 00:21:42,920
and on the fortitude of the workers.

277
00:21:43,580 --> 00:21:48,080
Many times their fingers were so numb they couldn't feel the tools in their hands.

278
00:21:55,400 --> 00:22:02,580
Eight hours a day, or ten or twelve, the brutal elements made progress of painful victory, but a lasting triumph.

279
00:22:02,740 --> 00:22:10,820
The steel shell of the dome covered with a thin white plastic sheeting would eventually trap the warmth of the stadium inside for the comfort of the fans.

280
00:22:10,821 --> 00:22:16,260
and the structure could stand under the strain of unlimited accumulations of snow on top.

281
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:24,100
More than 12,000 person years of work went into the effort,

282
00:22:24,740 --> 00:22:29,800
from laying foundations and hand-pounding massive support girders into alignment,

283
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:38,800
to installing 1,280 toilets, 9,000 light bulbs, and 67,000 spectator seats.

284
00:22:39,560 --> 00:22:43,220
The roof alone required a quarter million bolts,

285
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:47,680
but even that couldn't completely reinforce the public's confidence.

286
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:50,280
Not only did the roof have to work safely,

287
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:53,800
but it had to be perceived to be working safely

288
00:22:53,801 --> 00:22:56,760
so that people would have no concern at all

289
00:22:56,761 --> 00:23:00,800
when 8,000 tons of steel is rotating and moving around

290
00:23:00,801 --> 00:23:02,440
and the game carries on.

291
00:23:02,740 --> 00:23:06,860
We had reports in the papers from so-called scissayers.

292
00:23:06,861 --> 00:23:12,660
we had one very important gentleman who had predicted Kennedy's death and a few other events

293
00:23:12,661 --> 00:23:18,440
and he made headlines by predicting the roof would never open, it would never work.

294
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:27,340
The most emotional experience was the day we closed the roof, we built the roof open

295
00:23:27,341 --> 00:23:30,940
and then we moved panel one to the south end of the building.

296
00:23:30,941 --> 00:23:38,960
It moved very slowly, and we were all up there and touching the roof as it moved for the first time.

297
00:23:40,280 --> 00:23:46,800
The first day it moved, and I had a chance, the opportunity to ride shotgun.

298
00:23:47,300 --> 00:23:53,780
As she turned from north to south, it was a super day. It was a super day.

299
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:59,840
It's not every day you see a structure that's 500 feet start to emerge.

300
00:23:59,841 --> 00:24:03,920
It was like being born from the rest of the structure as it came out.

301
00:24:04,380 --> 00:24:07,960
Grown men were crying. I mean, this was a very magical moment.

302
00:24:08,320 --> 00:24:11,740
It was a very, very touching, moving experience

303
00:24:11,741 --> 00:24:15,780
because there were so many people who had said it wouldn't work,

304
00:24:16,260 --> 00:24:19,320
and this was the first manifestation that it would indeed work.

305
00:24:20,040 --> 00:24:22,580
Ironically, work was nearly completed

306
00:24:22,581 --> 00:24:26,340
when attentions finally turned to an important missing element.

307
00:24:26,600 --> 00:24:29,240
We ran a great contest naming Sky Dome here, too.

308
00:24:29,241 --> 00:24:31,360
We had 150,000 entries.

309
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:35,240
We had 12,000 different names, and we had 2,000 sky domes.

310
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:39,140
For us, it was either always the dome or the condome,

311
00:24:39,460 --> 00:24:44,840
because when we were designing the building, of course, it didn't have a name.

312
00:24:45,100 --> 00:24:51,540
We suggested condome to our client, and there was a lot of chuckling.

313
00:24:52,020 --> 00:24:55,840
So we narrowed it down with a group of 16 judges to the word sky dome.

314
00:24:55,841 --> 00:25:00,060
we had the premier of the day reach into a drum pulling out a name.

315
00:25:00,160 --> 00:25:02,200
Now the prize was two tickets for life.

316
00:25:02,760 --> 00:25:05,960
I leaned over to the premier and said, I hope you pick somebody really old.

317
00:25:07,400 --> 00:25:11,360
The winner was Kelly Watson, a young school teacher from Ontario

318
00:25:11,361 --> 00:25:16,060
who now enjoys lifetime seats valued at over $10,000 a year.

319
00:25:16,440 --> 00:25:20,220
But this big prize was only part of the cost overrun.

320
00:25:21,040 --> 00:25:25,280
The stadium didn't come in on budget because we kept adding things to it.

321
00:25:25,281 --> 00:25:27,900
We added the hotel, which was $110 million.

322
00:25:28,500 --> 00:25:29,800
We added a health club.

323
00:25:30,480 --> 00:25:32,740
Our initial boxes were only going to be 90.

324
00:25:33,240 --> 00:25:37,100
The demand said we should add more of those, so we got up to 161.

325
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:40,320
The total cost of the project was $570 million.

326
00:25:41,060 --> 00:25:46,380
Like a massive mechanical flower, Skydome blossomed into a multipurpose center

327
00:25:46,381 --> 00:25:49,000
designed to service the world's biggest crowds.

328
00:25:49,001 --> 00:25:55,300
It included 25 fast food outlets featuring the largest McDonald's restaurant in North America.

329
00:25:56,240 --> 00:26:04,900
A 350-room luxury hotel is part of the structure as well as a movie theater and a $13 million television studio.

330
00:26:05,460 --> 00:26:17,040
Three years and eight months to do everything in a building that went from being just a stadium into a multi-use facility with a hotel in it and a lot of other things.

331
00:26:17,041 --> 00:26:24,040
Considering the size of this job, there were very, very few accidents during the construction of the building.

332
00:26:24,180 --> 00:26:29,540
There were, unfortunately, some fatalities after it was finished on some clean-up work.

333
00:26:29,660 --> 00:26:33,300
There were two accidents, both of which killed two people.

334
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:37,400
Under the spinning and reticulating ceiling of Skydome,

335
00:26:37,600 --> 00:26:41,920
the energy and dreams of a city had been focused on one spectacular goal.

336
00:26:42,180 --> 00:26:45,620
But as opening day approached, everyone was secretly wondering,

337
00:26:45,621 --> 00:26:48,200
would the performance match the promise?

338
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:53,240
Engineers estimate that the Sky Dome

339
00:26:53,241 --> 00:26:56,480
will have a lifespan of approximately 300 years.

340
00:26:56,980 --> 00:26:59,200
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

341
00:27:00,580 --> 00:27:03,880
We now return to Modern Marvels.

342
00:27:05,680 --> 00:27:07,580
June 3rd, 1989.

343
00:27:08,080 --> 00:27:09,860
Toronto gathers for a first look

344
00:27:09,861 --> 00:27:12,080
at their new half-billion-dollar stadium

345
00:27:12,081 --> 00:27:13,980
with a grand opening celebration

346
00:27:13,981 --> 00:27:16,100
as large as the stadium itself.

347
00:27:17,940 --> 00:27:21,960
In an explosion of light and sound, Skydome was born.

348
00:27:23,460 --> 00:27:25,380
As part of the opening ceremonies,

349
00:27:25,540 --> 00:27:29,420
all the workers came in and paraded through the stadium

350
00:27:29,421 --> 00:27:33,740
and received a standing ovation from the crowd that was here.

351
00:27:34,460 --> 00:27:38,820
The iron workers, the electricians, the designers and plumbers

352
00:27:38,821 --> 00:27:41,280
were joined by over 5,000 performers,

353
00:27:41,281 --> 00:27:45,460
including a chorus line of 750 dancers.

354
00:27:46,620 --> 00:27:49,160
A joyous sea of humanity covered the field

355
00:27:49,161 --> 00:27:52,100
to usher in a new era in stadium spectaculars

356
00:27:52,101 --> 00:27:55,840
and see the dome open for the very first time.

357
00:27:55,841 --> 00:28:02,740
One, four, three, two, one.

358
00:28:21,031 --> 00:28:31,720
The crowd was so anxious for the experience, no one complained that a storm passing over the facility actually caused everyone to be drenched in a warm summer rain.

359
00:28:32,340 --> 00:28:35,378
Everyone got wet because it happened to be raining at the time.

360
00:28:35,379 --> 00:28:36,680
But what a wonderful event.

361
00:28:36,681 --> 00:28:40,300
I think the media would have been really critical had we not opened the roof.

362
00:28:40,800 --> 00:28:43,540
We also had six parachute jumpers outside,

363
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:46,100
and that would have been very difficult for them if we hadn't opened it.

364
00:28:46,460 --> 00:28:47,640
So we did open it.

365
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:50,340
When that roof opened up for the first time,

366
00:28:50,420 --> 00:28:54,420
really in the middle of a rainstorm, my goosebumps got goosebumps.

367
00:28:55,220 --> 00:29:00,600
There was a tear in my eye, and to this day I will never forget the emotion

368
00:29:00,601 --> 00:29:07,140
of trying to explain to my three sons how the Sky Dome was built,

369
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:12,160
and they were watching a bit of Canadian history and Toronto history really take place.

370
00:29:12,580 --> 00:29:17,660
Opening night of the Sky Dome was more than a celebration of a new era for sports in Toronto.

371
00:29:18,200 --> 00:29:21,960
It was an opportunity for the city to salute the men and women of the community

372
00:29:21,961 --> 00:29:23,960
who brought that vision to reality.

373
00:29:23,961 --> 00:29:35,280
I think in about 1988 there was a national competition that I read about to design a

374
00:29:35,281 --> 00:29:42,060
workers recognition mural for the Skydome and it was the first time that I'd seen a call for entry

375
00:29:42,061 --> 00:29:48,880
for a piece that was going to recognize workers the need and desire of the money men who built

376
00:29:48,881 --> 00:29:52,653
the stadium to recognize the men and women who built it.

377
00:29:52,654 --> 00:29:54,540
And I think that it's always

378
00:29:54,541 --> 00:29:57,780
been very important for workers to say, I built this.

379
00:29:57,781 --> 00:29:59,920
And here, they have the opportunity

380
00:29:59,921 --> 00:30:05,244
that their signatures are right on the piece. I have 1,800 names in this piece.

381
00:30:05,245 --> 00:30:06,120
We discovered

382
00:30:06,121 --> 00:30:12,220
a method, a photoemotion method, of actually getting their signatures and transferring

383
00:30:12,221 --> 00:30:15,838
them onto glass and sandblasting it into black flash glass.

384
00:30:15,839 --> 00:30:17,800
I think the day that this skydom

385
00:30:17,801 --> 00:30:22,040
opened and they came up and they looked for their name was a very exciting moment.

386
00:30:25,160 --> 00:30:29,780
Two nights later, it was the Blue Jays' turn to take flight in the new stadium,

387
00:30:30,200 --> 00:30:33,720
although preparations for the first pitch were far from complete.

388
00:30:34,120 --> 00:30:37,980
We were very excited about being the team to open the Sky Dome because it had such a

389
00:30:37,981 --> 00:30:42,236
build-up in terms of being the first convertible dome, so to speak.

390
00:30:42,237 --> 00:30:43,760
And I'll always remember

391
00:30:43,761 --> 00:30:49,920
coming out to the ballpark that day about 1 30 walking out here onto the field the tarp wasn't

392
00:30:49,921 --> 00:30:54,320
down there was no dirt on the field the fences weren't up and we said there's no way we're gonna

393
00:30:54,321 --> 00:30:58,560
be able to play this game tonight and sure enough about 4 30 came around and they were making

394
00:30:58,561 --> 00:31:01,920
progress they said hey batting practice is cancelled but we still think we can start the

395
00:31:01,921 --> 00:31:06,320
game on time about 6 30 they said well there's not gonna be an infield practice but we still

396
00:31:06,321 --> 00:31:11,520
think we can start the game on time about 7 15 they're putting down the dirt at home plate putting

397
00:31:11,521 --> 00:31:17,520
in home plate, the fences are just being completed putting up and sure enough at 7.35 the first pitch is thrown.

398
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:25,698
I happen to be the first batter facing Jimmy Key.

399
00:31:25,699 --> 00:31:29,520
I didn't swing at the first pitch because there was way too many flashbulbs going off.

400
00:31:29,521 --> 00:31:35,520
I had trouble discerning the ball from the light and eventually not a bat hit a double to left center field.

401
00:31:35,521 --> 00:31:42,200
So a small part of Skydome history, but one that I can say that I'm a part of forever, really.

402
00:31:45,640 --> 00:31:49,380
Great joy can come from the most humble of desires.

403
00:31:50,460 --> 00:31:53,800
To sit in the stands with a cold drink and a hot dog.

404
00:31:54,820 --> 00:31:57,540
To cheer the boys of summer as they run the bases.

405
00:31:58,620 --> 00:32:03,760
To glance up at the open sky and feel the fresh breeze.

406
00:32:03,761 --> 00:32:07,360
and consider that men may be able to go to the moon.

407
00:32:08,340 --> 00:32:11,140
The one real lasting accomplishment of a man

408
00:32:11,141 --> 00:32:13,800
is to be able to swap one out of the park.

409
00:32:27,420 --> 00:32:29,580
The pitcher's mound, which is interesting,

410
00:32:29,581 --> 00:32:35,180
is actually it's a big fiberglass tub floating on some water.

411
00:32:35,800 --> 00:32:37,300
And if we're going to go to football,

412
00:32:37,660 --> 00:32:39,780
we let the water out underneath this tub

413
00:32:39,781 --> 00:32:42,500
and the whole mound disappears down to the ground

414
00:32:42,501 --> 00:32:43,800
and then we'll put a cover over it.

415
00:32:44,360 --> 00:32:47,620
The design of the Sky Dome holds many secrets,

416
00:32:48,280 --> 00:32:51,260
but the biggest mysteries were buried at the site and uncovered

417
00:32:51,261 --> 00:32:54,280
before the first bulldozers plowed the soil.

418
00:32:54,500 --> 00:32:57,740
History told us that this was really the site of a huge garbage dump.

419
00:32:57,820 --> 00:32:59,120
There's probably a better way to put that,

420
00:32:59,121 --> 00:33:00,540
But that's really what it was.

421
00:33:01,060 --> 00:33:03,460
In the old days, they were trying to reclaim the waterfront.

422
00:33:03,520 --> 00:33:04,380
Of course, they needed real estate.

423
00:33:05,020 --> 00:33:09,160
And one way to do that was to dump anything, virtually anything that wasn't still alive,

424
00:33:09,260 --> 00:33:11,160
they would dump into the water on the water's edge.

425
00:33:11,700 --> 00:33:19,160
And so what we have here is a time capsule of what people were throwing out back in the, well, as early as 1850, 1860.

426
00:33:19,480 --> 00:33:23,560
Building permits from the city of Toronto demanded that the landfill site,

427
00:33:23,561 --> 00:33:28,840
which would support the Sky Dome, be studied for historical significance by an expert.

428
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:30,620
And you have to get a licensed archaeologist.

429
00:33:30,740 --> 00:33:32,960
It's not just some guy off the street with a shovel that does it.

430
00:33:33,020 --> 00:33:35,120
It's somebody who really knows what they're doing.

431
00:33:35,520 --> 00:33:40,480
I guess the most interesting of all the bottles is one that's not just visually interesting,

432
00:33:40,800 --> 00:33:43,660
but to your nose as well, because it's a mustard bottle.

433
00:33:44,060 --> 00:33:48,180
And we're talking about not yesterday's mustard, but mustard that's over 100 years old.

434
00:33:48,440 --> 00:33:54,680
And yet you can still smell traces of mustard as you try and crack the mustard jar.

435
00:33:55,340 --> 00:33:57,120
There are some tools, as you can see in here.

436
00:33:57,121 --> 00:33:59,400
But the question is, how did it get to be garbage?

437
00:33:59,520 --> 00:34:02,500
Did some guy say, I've had enough of this and throw it because he bought a brand new one?

438
00:34:02,920 --> 00:34:03,760
Or did he lose it?

439
00:34:04,200 --> 00:34:07,580
I mean, is he still wandering around somewhere saying, where did I put that thing?

440
00:34:08,120 --> 00:34:09,140
And this is a French cannon.

441
00:34:09,640 --> 00:34:10,980
We don't know how it got here.

442
00:34:11,740 --> 00:34:18,860
One extreme, perhaps, is that it came here in an attempt to bolster the armaments that we had when we were defending against the Americans.

443
00:34:19,240 --> 00:34:23,540
Many people don't realize that during the War of 1812, Toronto was attacked by the Americans.

444
00:34:23,541 --> 00:34:30,520
And for five or six days, the Americans were in charge of this community until they saw the price of gasoline and left.

445
00:34:30,780 --> 00:34:36,700
But this cannon conceivably came here in order to help us defend against the invasion.

446
00:34:37,340 --> 00:34:40,980
And now it's become part of your experience at Sky Dome.

447
00:34:41,060 --> 00:34:43,180
You just don't come here to watch a ball game or a football game.

448
00:34:43,500 --> 00:34:49,640
You can actually learn about the history of our community, which in my estimation is almost as important as winning a World Series.

449
00:34:49,641 --> 00:34:55,560
But for those who are more interested in today's games than in yesterday's garbage,

450
00:34:56,020 --> 00:34:59,600
SkyDome features a stupendous technological miracle.

451
00:35:00,420 --> 00:35:03,700
It's 30 feet high by 110 feet wide.

452
00:35:04,400 --> 00:35:07,320
It was until two years ago the largest screen in the world.

453
00:35:08,080 --> 00:35:10,920
The Jumbotron is a large video screen.

454
00:35:11,740 --> 00:35:13,260
It's not a solid piece.

455
00:35:13,680 --> 00:35:17,400
The board is actually made up of 67,200 of these things,

456
00:35:17,401 --> 00:35:19,040
and these are called Trinolites.

457
00:35:19,640 --> 00:35:22,380
And each one of these trino lights contains two picture elements.

458
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:28,380
And this element are three strips of phosphor.

459
00:35:29,440 --> 00:35:32,120
Blue, red, and green phosphor.

460
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:36,280
When you're very close to it, like if you're only three or four feet away from the screen,

461
00:35:36,340 --> 00:35:38,000
it just looks like a bunch of colored dots.

462
00:35:38,640 --> 00:35:42,580
Of course, jumbotrons are hundreds of feet away from the fans.

463
00:35:42,680 --> 00:35:47,000
When you get that far away, you can then see crisp, clean, clear video pictures.

464
00:35:47,001 --> 00:35:55,300
And if you take a magnifying glass and look at the television screen at your house, you'll see these same strips or dots of color.

465
00:35:56,180 --> 00:36:01,460
We can't show live action on the Jumbotron, and it's not something that's unique to this facility.

466
00:36:02,100 --> 00:36:05,240
All Major League Baseball teams have to abide by the rules.

467
00:36:05,800 --> 00:36:12,260
And the rules very clearly stipulate that no video screen is allowed to show live action while the game is in progress.

468
00:36:12,261 --> 00:36:16,460
If you as a batter are trying to concentrate on the ball coming from the pitcher's hand,

469
00:36:16,940 --> 00:36:22,320
you're distracted by a large video image of yourself in motion above the pitcher's head.

470
00:36:22,620 --> 00:36:25,840
It could distract your concentration on that 90-mile-an-hour fastball.

471
00:36:26,420 --> 00:36:30,400
Also, they don't want to see that over-the-shoulder shot from center field,

472
00:36:30,800 --> 00:36:33,080
which shows the catcher giving the signs,

473
00:36:33,660 --> 00:36:37,220
because then everyone in the ballpark would know what the next pitch is going to be.

474
00:36:37,221 --> 00:36:42,500
But the Sky Dome has used its Jumbotron screen for more than just baseball.

475
00:36:43,100 --> 00:36:47,600
There are over 250 events in this building on an annual basis.

476
00:36:48,060 --> 00:36:49,340
We've had auto races in the building.

477
00:36:49,480 --> 00:36:52,820
We've had bike races, dirt shows, concerts.

478
00:36:53,520 --> 00:36:59,560
And in addition, just recently, we showed the final game of the 1994 World Cup on the Jumbotron.

479
00:36:59,561 --> 00:37:05,480
and 17,000 local fans came down to the building

480
00:37:05,481 --> 00:37:10,160
to watch the final game between Brazil and Italy on the Jumbotron.

481
00:37:11,180 --> 00:37:14,140
Ironically, one of the biggest events at the Sky Dome

482
00:37:14,141 --> 00:37:15,780
didn't happen at the Sky Dome.

483
00:37:16,560 --> 00:37:21,500
October 1992, the Toronto Blue Jays captured the World Series

484
00:37:21,501 --> 00:37:23,780
in a triumphant final game in Atlanta.

485
00:37:23,781 --> 00:37:29,440
But in their hometown, nearly 60,000 fans packed the stadium to watch the win.

486
00:37:29,560 --> 00:37:32,420
on the giant Jumbotron screen.

487
00:37:34,580 --> 00:37:38,020
It is a surprising twist to the end of the 20th century

488
00:37:38,021 --> 00:37:41,240
that spectacular arenas can achieve popularity

489
00:37:41,241 --> 00:37:44,900
simply by showcasing a spectacular television set.

490
00:37:45,740 --> 00:37:50,900
But the Toronto Sky Dome has discovered that spectacle has a powerful allure,

491
00:37:51,500 --> 00:37:56,000
whether it's a live contest or simply a stupendous presentation.

492
00:37:59,560 --> 00:38:05,040
The first World Series ever played outside of the United States was played in the Toronto Sky Dome.

493
00:38:05,860 --> 00:38:07,540
Modern Marvels will return.

494
00:38:12,940 --> 00:38:16,140
We now return to Modern Marvels.

495
00:38:18,120 --> 00:38:22,360
It takes about 20 minutes to open the roof once you've pushed the button,

496
00:38:22,960 --> 00:38:26,700
but there's work beforehand before you can get to that point.

497
00:38:26,701 --> 00:38:30,880
You have inspections. All roof areas have to be inspected.

498
00:38:31,500 --> 00:38:39,860
It takes five people, anywhere from four to six hours, and they look at every area, every catwalk, every tunnel area.

499
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:44,800
They're looking for anything that might interrupt or cause the roof not to function properly.

500
00:38:45,460 --> 00:38:50,360
Every time the Sky Dome opens, there is an investment of time and money.

501
00:38:50,640 --> 00:38:53,920
I'd say we're running between $1,000 and $5,000.

502
00:38:53,921 --> 00:38:57,140
You open the roof up for two, three hours.

503
00:38:57,540 --> 00:39:00,220
The sun gets absorbed into the concrete and the seats and everything.

504
00:39:00,300 --> 00:39:01,200
You shut the roof later.

505
00:39:01,480 --> 00:39:05,640
You spend the next eight hours trying to get rid of that heat that you've collected while the roof was open.

506
00:39:06,220 --> 00:39:13,000
The real trick in operating the 10,000 metric ton dome is being able to predict perfect baseball weather.

507
00:39:13,800 --> 00:39:16,260
We're in the central control room for roof operation.

508
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:22,800
It's in this room we have the equipment that actually monitors and operates the roof,

509
00:39:22,801 --> 00:39:30,300
as well as a radar system that allows us to go online with our weather company,

510
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:34,920
our weather contractor, and decide whether or not we want to operate.

511
00:39:35,360 --> 00:39:37,940
This particular screen is the Toronto radar,

512
00:39:38,640 --> 00:39:40,480
and what you see, the different colours,

513
00:39:40,800 --> 00:39:45,400
is the different amount of precipitation in this actual rain area.

514
00:39:45,401 --> 00:39:48,660
So the darker the colour, the more water you're getting.

515
00:39:48,661 --> 00:39:54,860
and it's giving us a 40% chance of rain and various other information that we need to know.

516
00:39:55,580 --> 00:39:59,640
Once the decision is made to open the dome, the process is automatic.

517
00:40:00,100 --> 00:40:02,960
Okay, we've finished the inspection on the east and the west sides.

518
00:40:03,120 --> 00:40:05,520
We'll be ready to open in 10 seconds. Stand by.

519
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:15,280
Opening the largest retractable dome stadium on earth

520
00:40:15,281 --> 00:40:19,860
requires the coordinated effort of 52 separate 100-horsepower engines,

521
00:40:20,500 --> 00:40:22,680
pulling the enormous panels along tracks.

522
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:56,960
These are the main indicator lights for when we're operating the roof.

523
00:40:56,961 --> 00:41:02,840
Once we've pushed the open or close buttons, which are here, a series of operations happen

524
00:41:02,841 --> 00:41:06,420
up on the roof and you want to see things happen in a specific order.

525
00:41:06,421 --> 00:41:08,360
That's what these lights let you know.

526
00:41:08,361 --> 00:41:14,140
Up here we have our position, skew and speed indicators.

527
00:41:14,141 --> 00:41:19,820
The speed of the panels is 70 feet per minute, and skew is the difference in the position

528
00:41:19,821 --> 00:41:22,980
on one side of the panel to the other side.

529
00:41:40,769 --> 00:41:45,560
The opening of the Sky Dome is an elegant and mesmerizing technological dance.

530
00:41:45,800 --> 00:41:51,740
Like many objects of enormous beauty, the Sky Dome's poetry of form and graceful movements

531
00:41:51,741 --> 00:41:56,740
suggest a lightness, an almost playful spirit in concrete and steel.

532
00:41:58,980 --> 00:42:02,220
That spirit was, in fact, built into the structure,

533
00:42:02,620 --> 00:42:07,920
a solid reflection of the team spirit which conceived and constructed Toronto's dome.

534
00:42:07,921 --> 00:42:21,921
Toronto is a Native American word for gathering place.

535
00:42:23,800 --> 00:42:27,700
As a civic project, Sky Dome magnifies the meaning of the city's name.

536
00:42:29,120 --> 00:42:35,180
This dome stadium is a modern marvel for its size, its design,

537
00:42:35,181 --> 00:42:38,240
and the powerful gatherings it has hosted,

538
00:42:39,200 --> 00:42:42,740
particularly in 1993 when the Toronto Blue Jays

539
00:42:42,741 --> 00:42:46,180
took the World Series crown on the Skydon field.

540
00:42:48,680 --> 00:42:52,500
Still there are people in the world who have never been to a baseball game.

541
00:42:53,260 --> 00:42:55,480
They've never taken a seventh inning stretch

542
00:42:55,481 --> 00:42:58,300
or called the umpire a bum.

543
00:42:59,640 --> 00:43:03,840
But anyone who has been to the park knows something about the human condition

544
00:43:03,841 --> 00:43:07,240
that can never be written on paper or put in a bottle.

545
00:43:08,180 --> 00:43:11,640
It is to roar with the pack, snarling at one instant,

546
00:43:11,740 --> 00:43:16,300
then cheering the next as a nine-ounce ball carries the crowd to heaven.

547
00:43:20,280 --> 00:43:23,420
Stadiums are not just places where people go for the games.

548
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:27,600
They do not simply house the playing fields in a confined space.

549
00:43:28,360 --> 00:43:32,140
Instead, stadiums are a way of keeping the everyday world out.

550
00:43:32,141 --> 00:43:35,440
to maintain the purity of the sport.

551
00:43:36,800 --> 00:43:40,240
Here is sanctuary and communion that nourishes the soul

552
00:43:40,241 --> 00:43:45,440
and allows the common person to face yet another week of the real world.

553
00:43:46,600 --> 00:43:50,840
When a city builds a stadium, it is creating a multifaceted jewel

554
00:43:50,841 --> 00:43:53,720
through which the world can see the community's best nature.

555
00:43:55,580 --> 00:44:00,140
Wanting to dome this jewel in a protective top seems an obvious endeavor

556
00:44:00,141 --> 00:44:05,900
designed to defy the elements, to allow the games to go on all year round.

557
00:44:07,080 --> 00:44:11,540
But the construction of a dome that opens and exposes its prize inside

558
00:44:11,541 --> 00:44:15,060
is a wonderful gift from Toronto to the world.

559
00:44:17,140 --> 00:44:20,740
Over 50 million people have been inside Skydome

560
00:44:20,741 --> 00:44:24,200
and once touched by this magic, you never leave.

561
00:44:25,140 --> 00:44:28,600
Part of you remains, joining the countless other spirits

562
00:44:28,601 --> 00:44:33,560
whose song is the chorus of the universal passion of sports.

